  "notes": [
   "MABS needs no agent on vCenter or the ESXi hosts. It talks to vCenter over HTTPS on TCP 443, using a VMware account that holds a role with only the privileges backup needs. Where vCenter manages the ESXi hosts, add vCenter, not the hosts, to the protection group. For HTTPS, import the VMware CA certificate on the MABS server.",
   "MABS reads the VM data from the ESXi hosts over TCP 443 and TCP 902 and keeps it first on local disk, in a storage pool that can be direct attached storage, a Fibre Channel SAN or iSCSI storage. A vCenter VM folder can be protected as one unit and new VMs in it are picked up daily.",
   "The protection group sets the short-term disk retention. The MARS agent that runs on the MABS server then backs up the MABS disks to the Recovery Services vault.",
   "Online protection sends recovery points from MABS to the vault on the schedule you set. There is no time limit on retention; the limit is 9999 recovery points per protected instance.",
   "For recovery you can restore the whole VM or, for Windows VMs, files and folders without restoring the whole VM. Recovery goes through MABS.",
   "Machines outside VMware, such as a physical Windows file server, run the MABS protection agent and back up to the same MABS server. Linux VMs on VMware are protected as a whole VM, with file-consistent snapshots only.",
   "Optional: with a private endpoint on the vault, connect the on-premises network to the virtual network that hosts the endpoint, so the MARS agent's backup traffic goes through it.",
   "Optional: for a large first backup, offline seeding copies the initial backup to disks that are transferred to Azure.",
   "The MABS database, DPMDB, holds the MABS configuration and backup data. Back it up to Azure too, so any MABS server registered in the vault can recover it."
  ]
